 },
 "finding": "Measured through the reconstruction's own masks and registration, on simulated images of the 2022 geometry with motion put in through the Doppler-time mapping, the six-second images do carry a vibration, but not as a boxcar average: read against its motionless twin, a lone bright point's image moves by 0.062 to 45 times the boxcar value, depending on frequency (at 2 mm/s and 0.5 Hz, 0.129 px where the boxcar gives 0.0085 px). The pair registration then catches only part of the shift between its two images: 3% to 46% for the bright point and 0.6% to 2.8% for a 20 m block of texture, where that shift is resolvable. At 2 mm/s, some 40,000 times Giza's microseisms, the pair shifts change by at most 0.011 px for the point, about the 0.01 px rounding step, and 0.0017 px for texture. The moving point passes 0 of the first gate's windows at every frequency and both speeds, its trace a line; with clutter or texture the traces large enough to measure are at most 0.06 as wide as they are long, under the gate's 0.1, while still texture alone passes 24 windows."
}